      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14152741/assets/1f56006dd06fab3bbc736c45ac6e9d65a401b88e0a9eb8a5db404fb67b89977e.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14152741/assets/f393d0facc98fe365e2c622e24223d6045b48b490937ba6e2f25d86d7eb4810f.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-8{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{2}-(-8{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[2{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}32\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
