    "q-126737": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126737",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14152741/assets/fb6a49b2172b3a60676ef74a244298eff05ffc65be6feca089c4a953757f2e59.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14152741/assets/d816d9153e22dd109e20e3e387f6a0028e3f6a9eaf1d284dfae8b5fdf54cfc6e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
